<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aimery of Cyprus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1205">1205</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Aimery of Lusignan · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-lusignan">House of Lusignan</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aimery of Lusignan was the first king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and later also king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. He succeeded his brother Guy as ruler of Cyprus in 1194, obtained a royal crown from the Emperor Henry VI and was crowned king in 1197. In the same year he married Queen Isabella I of Jerusalem and became king of Jerusalem, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> until his death in 1205.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aimery-of-cyprus.jpg" alt="Aimery of Cyprus" width="320" height="275" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gustave Schlumberger · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aimery_II_de_Lusignan.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Aimery of Lusignan was a younger son of Hugh VIII of Lusignan, a nobleman from Poitou, born before 1155. After joining a rebellion in 1168 against King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a>, Aimery went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>. He married Eschiva of Ibelin, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-of-ibelin">Baldwin of Ibelin</a>, one of the kingdom's most powerful nobles. By 1182 he was constable of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, and his brother Guy became king through his marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sibylla-queen-of-jerusalem">Queen Sibylla</a> in 1186. Aimery was one of the commanders at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Battle of Hattin</a> on 4 July 1187, where he was captured; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> freed him in early 1188.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Aimery supported Guy at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> from 1189 and stayed loyal when most barons held that Guy had lost the crown at Sibylla's death in 1190. Isabella I married Count Henry II of Champagne, and Guy was compensated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a> had conquered. Guy made Aimery constable of Cyprus.</p><p>When Guy died in late 1194 the island's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> elected Aimery lord. The treasury was almost empty, and he persuaded his vassals one by one to give up rents and lands; by the end of his reign revenue was at least 200,000 bezants.</p><p>Aimery wanted a royal crown, to guard against Byzantine attempts to recover the island and to end any idea that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> depended on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. He offered Emperor Henry VI his homage, and the chancellor Conrad of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/querfurt">Querfurt</a> crowned him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicosia">Nicosia</a> in September 1197. Henry of Champagne died that month, and the barons of Jerusalem offered Isabella, now a widow, to Aimery, whose first wife had died. The marriage, or a betrothal, took place in October, and he became king of Jerusalem.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He kept the two kingdoms separate, sent Cypriot troops to the mainland and spent most of his reign there. He sought to codify the laws of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, which produced the Livre au roi. After surviving an assassination attempt in 1198, he tried to banish Isabella's seneschal, Ralph of Saint-Omer. He signed two truces with the Ayyubid sultan al-Adil I, the second securing the Christian coastline from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>. He died of food poisoning on 1 April 1205. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> passed to his son Hugh I, and Isabella kept Jerusalem. Mary Nickerson Hardwicke calls him politically astute, and Steven Runciman says he was not a great king but had a valuable political wisdom.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aimery of Cyprus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His brother <b>Aimery</b> obtained a crown from the Emperor Henry VI and was crowned king in 1197.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-cyprus">Kingdom of Cyprus</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Poitou, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Acre, in what is now <a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.65,-0.25&amp;year=1195" aria-label="Poitou on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Aimery of Cyprus"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4985 6131h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4985 6131h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.65,-0.25&amp;year=1195">What was here in 1195? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
